<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Klaipėda Region</h1><p class="st-meta">1920 CE – 1939 CE · capital Klaipėda</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Memel Territory, detached from Germany after the First World War, seized by Lithuania in 1923 and annexed by Hitler in 1939.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> separated the land north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neman">Neman</a>, including the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Memel</a> (Klaipėda), from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, placing it under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> in 1920 with a French administration. In January 1923 Lithuanian irregulars staged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda-revolt">Klaipėda Revolt</a>, and the Allies accepted Lithuanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda-convention">Klaipėda Convention</a> of 1924, the region was an autonomous part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-lithuania-1918-1940">Republic of Lithuania</a>, with its own parliament and directorate. Most inhabitants were German-speaking or bilingual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lietuvininkai">Lietuvininkai</a>, and conflicts between German parties and the Lithuanian governor were constant. The port was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania's</a> only outlet to the sea.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Nazi groups grew strong in the 1930s. In March 1939 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> issued an ultimatum, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a> ceded the region, which was annexed to the Reich on 23 March 1939. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lithuanian-soviet-socialist-republic">Lithuanian Soviet Socialist Republic</a>, and its German population fled or was expelled.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/klaipeda-region.jpg" alt="Klaipėda Region" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Klaip%C4%97da_Region.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1920 CE – 1939 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Klaipėda</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Lithuanian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/lithuania">Lithuania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Memel Territory, Memelland, Klaipėdos kraštas</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1930&amp;focus=klaipeda-region" aria-label="Klaipėda Region on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5141 278 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Klaipėda Region lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5461 534L5300 622M5461 534L5526 548M5461 534L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5300 622h0M5526 548h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5461 534h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5461 534h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Klaipėda. <a href="/atlas?year=1930&amp;focus=klaipeda-region">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
